From cf4cf0272a45fefa66a2d4068227061db83bdd18 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ms178 <m.seyfarth@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 29 Aug 2026 16:57:09 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/3] x86: only fold indirect calls through memory when the IR
 value is a slot load

try_resolve_indirect_call_global's defensive fallback resolved the
func_ptr VALUE chain (GlobalAddr/GEP/Copy/Cast) and then emitted the
memory-indirect form 'call *sym+off(%rip)'. That form reads the target
through sym, but a value-resolved chain IS the target: for
GlobalAddr(add7) the correct lowering is a direct 'call add7', while
'call *add7(%rip)' interprets add7's first eight code bytes as a call
destination and jumps into the middle of an instruction.

This regressed every regression-corpus test that calls through a
coalesced/propagated function pointer (pic_extern_addr SIGSEGV,
fnptr_param_assign, fnptr_return_declarator, kr_variadic_function_pointer,
pointers_mem, tail_call_frameless, ...): 10 fails on main at 4630de02,
0 after this fix. The Load-based arm keeps its semantics: a loaded
slot (pv_ops+off) is the one case where the memory-indirect ff 15
form is both correct and paravirt-patchable, so it is preserved
verbatim via an explicit MemorySlot/Direct distinction.
